  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure over the Gulf of Maine departs as high \npressure builds over the area.\n\nThe former nor'easter, now weak low pressure, over the Gulf of Maine \ncontinues to depart late tonight. High pressure centered near West \nVirginia will build east late tonight and into Wednesday. \n\nClouds over the area will gradually scatter out late tonight. With \nabundant low level moisture over the area and winds becoming nearly \ncalm, patchy fog will develop during the early morning hours. Fog \nwill lift and dissipate Wednesday morning, giving way to mostly \nsunny skies. Mild with slightly above normal temperatures on \nWednesday with highs in the mid to upper 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor tidal flooding continues in the back bays of New \nJersey and Delaware through tonight.\n\nAnother round of tidal flooding is expected to occur through \nthis afternoon and potentially into early Wednesday morning's \nhigh tide cycle. With water continuing to slowly drain out of\nthe back bays with light offshore flow, the most prone areas of\nminor tidal flooding will be in the back bays of Barnegat Bay,\nRehoboth Bay, Jefferson Creek and Little Assawoman Bay. As a\nresult, the Coastal Flood Advisories for Ocean County, NJ and\nSussex County, DE have been extended until 9 PM this evening.\nWaters should continue to recede below advisory levels on\nWednesday\n\nOutside of these areas, a few spots may reach spotty minor with\ntonight's high tide cycle, otherwise, no further tidal flooding\nis expected elsewhere.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled weather possible Friday through\nSunday.\n\nA cold front is forecast to move across the area late Friday into \nFriday night. As it moves into the area, there will be a chance of \nshowers. However, as the vorticity impulse aloft weakens, the \nshowers may dissipate for many areas as the move eastward. However, \nadditional rain fall is expected to move back into the area Saturday \nnight into Sunday. An area of low pressure is forecast to develop \nalong the front as it stalls to our south Saturday, then move \nnorthward across the area through Sunday. Showers will increase \nSaturday night into Sunday as a couple of short wave/vorticity \nimpulses move into the area, along with the surface low\npressure.",
